    id: "motion",
    title: "Going on foot and by transport: идти, ехать",
    blurb: "Say where you are going and how: on foot, or by bus, car or train.",
    deck: "Getting around",
    body: `
<p>English "go" covers walking to the store and flying to Moscow. Russian splits it in two: <b>идти</b> means going on foot, <b>ехать</b> means going by any vehicle. The choice depends on how you travel, not on how far: a two-minute bus ride is still <b>ехать</b>.</p>
<h3>идти and ехать</h3>
<div class="tbl"><table><tr><th>Who</th><th>on foot</th><th>by transport</th></tr><tr><td class="k">я</td><td class="k">иду</td><td class="k">еду</td></tr><tr><td class="k">ты</td><td class="k">идёшь</td><td class="k">едешь</td></tr><tr><td class="k">он, она</td><td class="k">идёт</td><td class="k">едет</td></tr><tr><td class="k">мы</td><td class="k">идём</td><td class="k">едем</td></tr><tr><td class="k">вы</td><td class="k">идёте</td><td class="k">едете</td></tr><tr><td class="k">они</td><td class="k">идут</td><td class="k">едут</td></tr></table></div>
{{ex:Я иду домой.|yah ee-DOO dah-MOY|I'm walking home.}}
{{ex:Я еду на работу.|yah YEH-doo nah rah-BOH-too|I'm going to work (by transport).}}
<p>Both verbs take the same endings as other first-conjugation verbs, with the stress at the end for <b>идти</b>. Going to another city is always <b>ехать</b>: nobody walks from Boston to Moscow.</p>
<h3>Куда? Where to</h3>
<p>For direction, ask <b>куда</b> and use <b>в</b> or <b>на</b> with the accusative case: <b>в Москву</b>, <b>в школу</b>, <b>на работу</b>. For being somewhere, ask <b>где</b> and use the prepositional: <b>в школе</b>. <b>Домой</b> means "home" as a direction; <b>дома</b> means "at home".</p>
{{ex:Куда вы идёте?|koo-DAH vih ee-DYOH-tih|Where are you going? (formal)}}
{{ex:Мы едем в Москву.|mih YEH-dihm v mahs-KVOO|We're going to Moscow.}}
<h3>How: на + the vehicle</h3>
<p>To say how you travel, use <b>на</b> and the prepositional: <b>на автобусе</b>, <b>на машине</b>, <b>на метро</b> (метро never changes).</p>
{{ex:Она едет на автобусе.|ah-NAH YEH-diht nah ahf-TOH-boo-sih|She's going by bus.}}
<h3>ходить and ездить: habits and round trips</h3>
<p><b>Идти</b> and <b>ехать</b> describe one trip in one direction, often right now. For going regularly, or for a trip there and back, Russian uses a second pair: <b>ходить</b> (on foot) and <b>ездить</b> (by transport). Recognize them for now. <b>Я ездил в Казань</b> means you went there and are back now.</p>
{{ex:Каждый день я хожу на работу.|KAHZH-dihy dyehn' yah khah-ZHOO nah rah-BOH-too|I walk to work every day.}}
{{ex:Я ездил в Казань.|yah YEHZ-deel f kah-ZAHN'|I went to Kazan. (a man)}}
<div class="tip"><b>Most common mistake:</b> using идти for every "go". <b>Я иду в Москву</b> (from Boston) → <b>Я еду в Москву</b>. Read the Cyrillic first, and use the respelling only to check the stress.</div>
`,
