    body: `
<p>English says "<i>at</i> two", "<i>on</i> Monday", "<i>in</i> May": three different words. Russian uses <b>в</b> for all three, but the case after it changes.</p>
<h3>What time is it?</h3>
<p>Ask <b>Который час?</b> or <b>Сколько времени?</b> The answer is a number plus <b>час</b>, which changes like <b>рубль</b>: <b>час</b> alone for one, <b>два, три, четыре часа</b>, and <b>пять часов</b> up to twelve.</p>
{{ex:Который час?|kah-TOH-rihy chahs|What time is it?}}
{{ex:Сейчас три часа.|sihy-CHAHS tree chih-SAH|It's three o'clock now.}}
<h3>At what time: в + the time</h3>
<p>Put <b>в</b> before the time, and keep the same <b>час, часа, часов</b>: <b>в час</b>, <b>в два часа</b>, <b>в пять часов</b>.</p>
{{ex:Урок в пять часов.|oo-ROHK f pyaht' chih-SOHF|The class is at five o'clock.}}
<h3>On which day: в + accusative</h3>
<p>Days take <b>в</b> and the accusative case. Feminine days change <b>-а</b> to <b>-у</b>: <b>в среду</b>, <b>в пятницу</b>, <b>в субботу</b>. Masculine days look the same as always: <b>в понедельник</b>. Before <b>вторник</b>, в becomes <b>во</b>: <b>во вторник</b>. Days and months are not capitalized.</p>
{{ex:В субботу я буду дома.|f soo-BOH-too yah BOO-doo DOH-mah|On Saturday I'll be at home.}}
<h3>Parts of the day: one word</h3>
<p><b>Утром</b>, <b>днём</b>, <b>вечером</b>, <b>ночью</b> mean "in the morning", "in the afternoon", "in the evening", "at night". They are single words, with no <b>в</b>.</p>
{{ex:Вечером я читаю.|VYEH-chih-rahm yah chee-TAH-yoo|In the evening I read.}}
<h3>In which month: в + prepositional</h3>
<p>Months take <b>в</b> and the prepositional case: <b>в январе</b>, <b>в марте</b>, <b>в мае</b>.</p>
{{ex:Мой день рождения в мае.|moy dyehn' rahzh-DYEH-nee-yah v MAH-yih|My birthday is in May.}}
<h3>Dates, to recognize</h3>
<p>A date is an ordinal number ("first", "second") in the neuter form plus the month with a new ending: <b>первое мая</b>, "the first of May". Just recognize dates for now.</p>
{{ex:Сегодня первое мая.|sih-VOHD-nyah PYEHR-vah-yih MAH-yah|Today is the first of May.}}
<div class="tip"><b>Most common mistake:</b> using the "where" ending for days. <b>в понедельнике</b> → <b>в понедельник</b>, and <b>два часы</b> → <b>два часа</b>. Read the Cyrillic first, and use the respelling only to check the stress.</div>
`,
